WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.274
Can vision language models learn intuitive physics from
interaction?

00:00:05.724 --> 00:00:13.620
From the Institute for Human-Centered AI at Helmholtz Munich,
published at ICML 2026.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:20.454
Note the question mark in the title. This is a negative
result, and it is reported carefully.

00:00:21.804 --> 00:00:31.044
Intuitive physics is the fast, implicit sense of how objects
behave — the expectations you never have to reason about.

00:00:31.494 --> 00:00:36.126
Support: something without enough of a base under it falls
over.

00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:44.376
Solidity: two solid things cannot occupy the same place, so a
ball dropped on a table stops at the table.

00:00:44.826 --> 00:00:56.322
Permanence: something hidden behind an occluder still exists
and should come out the other side. Continuity: it travels a

00:00:56.772 --> 00:01:10.140
Infants show these expectations in the first year of life.
Vision language models largely do not — they underperform on

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:19.434
Prior work found that supervised fine-tuning makes models good
at whatever task you trained on, and does not transfer.

00:01:19.884 --> 00:01:28.356
The suspected reason is shortcut learning. Instead of the
rule, the model finds a surface cue that happens to predict

00:01:28.806 --> 00:01:39.678
Suppose most training towers are three blocks tall. A small
offset almost always means stable, so the rule 'small offset,

00:01:40.128 --> 00:01:48.936
Change the tower height and that cue breaks. The same offset
is now unstable, and the model still says stable.

00:01:49.386 --> 00:01:57.090
The rule and the shortcut agree on the training data and
disagree off it. That is the failure the paper is trying to

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:06.960
A long tradition in cognitive science holds that people learn
robust physical concepts by acting on the world, not by

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:16.674
Children experiment. They stack things and knock them over,
and learn most from trying and failing to predict what their

00:02:17.124 --> 00:02:24.108
Supervised fine-tuning is the passive case: the model is shown
correct answers it did not produce.

00:02:24.558 --> 00:02:31.470
Reinforcement learning is the active case: the model acts, and
the environment tells it what happened.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:43.728
So the hypothesis is direct. Train a VLM by interaction and it
should acquire generalisable physical intuitions in a way that

00:02:45.078 --> 00:02:58.038
The setup is deliberately minimal. A tower of two to four
coloured cubes, rendered at 256 by 256 from a fixed camera in

00:02:58.488 --> 00:03:07.224
The camera angle and the block sizes never change, so the
model can in principle learn a stable mapping from pixels to

00:03:07.674 --> 00:03:14.250
Every block is perfectly stacked except one, which is
displaced left or right.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:24.012
That single offset is the whole task variable. It determines
whether the tower is stable, and it determines the correct

00:03:24.462 --> 00:03:31.902
Two datasets: one where the displaced block sits on top of the
tower, and one where it sits on the floor beside it.

00:03:33.252 --> 00:03:36.996
From those two datasets they build four tasks.

00:03:37.446 --> 00:03:43.278
Binary stability asks a yes-or-no question: is this tower
stable?

00:03:43.728 --> 00:03:49.920
X-only asks for a single integer — how far to move the block
to make the tower more stable.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:56.082
X-y adds a second integer, moving the block sideways and up
onto the tower.

00:03:56.532 --> 00:04:16.140
The design is the point. These tasks share visual statistics,
share the same physics engine, and in some cases require

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:22.182
If a model learns the physics rather than the task, transfer
between these should be easy.

00:04:23.532 --> 00:04:29.316
The reward is shaped, because a single scalar has to teach a
continuous action.

00:04:29.766 --> 00:04:36.798
Answers that cannot be parsed get minus five. Answers that put
the block below the floor get minus four.

00:04:37.248 --> 00:04:44.904
For parseable answers, the reward is a Gaussian in the
distance from the optimal position — closer is better.

00:04:45.354 --> 00:04:59.970
But the height of that Gaussian depends on the outcome. If the
resulting tower is unstable, the peak is small and the whole

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:08.580
So the reward encodes both 'how close were you' and 'did it
actually work', and the step between them is where the physics

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:16.386
Interaction here is one-step reinforcement learning with
Group-Relative Policy Optimization.

00:05:16.836 --> 00:05:25.140
For each image and prompt, the model generates sixteen
completions. Each gets a reward from that shaped function.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:37.182
The advantage is the reward minus the group mean, divided by
the group standard deviation. Above-average completions get

00:05:37.632 --> 00:05:45.744
That is what makes it group-relative: there is no learned
value network, because the other fifteen samples serve as the

00:05:46.194 --> 00:06:02.946
The comparison arm is supervised fine-tuning on optimal
actions — the offline analogue. Both use identical LoRA

00:06:04.296 --> 00:06:11.784
The first hypothesis was that interaction would beat
supervision on held-out instances of the trained task.

00:06:12.234 --> 00:06:26.418
Both methods hit ceiling. Binary stability: 0.94 for GRPO,
0.97 for SFT. X-only: both reach 20.00, the maximum.

00:06:26.868 --> 00:06:36.324
On the hardest task, x-y side block, SFT is actually ahead —
19.86 against 17.31.

00:06:36.774 --> 00:06:44.214
So there is no within-task advantage from interaction. If
anything the supervised model is marginally better.

00:06:44.664 --> 00:06:51.144
This is important context for what follows: both models
genuinely learned to do the thing.

00:06:52.494 --> 00:06:59.502
The second hypothesis was that interaction would generalise
better. This matrix is the answer.

00:06:59.952 --> 00:07:05.688
The diagonal is near ceiling — every model does the task it
was trained on.

00:07:06.138 --> 00:07:19.098
Off the diagonal, almost everything collapses to the baseline
or below. Negative numbers mean the model is emitting answers

00:07:19.548 --> 00:07:38.100
There is a little structure. The model trained on x-y side
block gets 5.4 on x-only side block, which is unsurprising

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:50.622
But x-only top block to x-only side block — the same task, the
same action space, just a larger displacement — gives 0.04.

00:07:51.072 --> 00:07:58.128
And the same matrix computed for the supervised models looks
the same. Interaction bought nothing.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:05.814
They also test on a hundred photographs of real wooden block
towers from an independent dataset.

00:08:06.264 --> 00:08:14.352
The model trained on binary stability transfers a little:
sixty percent, against a chance baseline of fifty.

00:08:14.802 --> 00:08:27.642
Models trained on the other three tasks are at or below chance
— the x-y model reaches thirty-one percent, which is worse

00:08:28.092 --> 00:08:31.236
Every model is below the human average.

00:08:31.686 --> 00:08:40.662
So the small amount of sim-to-real transfer that exists comes
from having trained on the same task, not from having learned

00:08:42.012 --> 00:08:50.172
This is the most interesting analysis in the paper, and it
separates two things that failure usually confuses.

00:08:50.622 --> 00:09:00.270
The distinction is competence versus performance. Maybe the
model knows the answer and simply cannot produce it in the

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:09.864
So they train linear probes on the hidden activations at each
layer, and ask whether tower stability and the block offset

00:09:10.314 --> 00:09:24.738
Both are, at high accuracy, from the earliest layers onward —
and crucially, in the base model, before any fine-tuning.

00:09:25.188 --> 00:09:32.412
So the representation is there and the behaviour is not. The
models have the information and do not recruit it.

00:09:32.862 --> 00:09:45.870
The authors are candid that stability may be decodable through
a pixel-level shortcut — a few pixels along the centre line of

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:59.460
The obvious objection is that this is not really interaction.
One-step RL means the model never sees the consequence of its

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:11.718
So they built a triplet version: the model sees a frame, is
told an action was taken, sees the result, another action, a

00:10:12.168 --> 00:10:14.760
The model learns that version fine.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:24.474
But it does not generalise to the single-image version of the
same task — and the single-image model does not generalise to

00:10:24.924 --> 00:10:34.884
Same visual statistics, same reward function, same physics.
Still no transfer. Seeing the consequence of an action did not

00:10:36.234 --> 00:10:43.530
The ablations are extensive, and this is what makes the
negative result credible rather than a single failed run.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:52.572
A different model family: generalisation is even worse. The
32-billion parameter model shows none at all.

00:10:53.022 --> 00:10:59.430
A different RL algorithm, GSPO with sequence-level
optimisation: same pattern.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:12.624
Longer training, up to forty-eight thousand steps: past ten
thousand the model overfits harder to the exact reward

00:11:13.074 --> 00:11:29.898
And one genuine positive. Trained on two tasks in sequence,
the GRPO model retains both, while the supervised model

00:11:31.248 --> 00:11:42.120
To summarise: both hypotheses failed. Interaction gave no
within-task advantage and no generalisation advantage over

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:53.658
The probing analysis says the models represent the relevant
physical quantities and simply do not use them, which points

00:11:54.108 --> 00:11:59.892
Three caveats, and they matter more than usual because this is
a negative result.

00:12:00.342 --> 00:12:13.134
Interaction here means one-step reinforcement learning with a
scalar reward. That is a long way from a child manipulating

00:12:13.584 --> 00:12:19.224
The models are four-bit quantised with LoRA adapters, not
fully fine-tuned.

00:12:19.674 --> 00:12:24.546
And the environment has essentially one degree of freedom by
design.

00:12:24.996 --> 00:12:43.668
So the claim supported is narrow: this operationalisation of
interaction, at this scale, does not confer generalisation.

